Two corrections to the overlay section, both about what the channel actually
guarantees.

"Three constraints" is now four. PR #306 added a refusal of
stdlib_module_completeness from overlays — since INV-buzab that field is the
single grant of confirmability, so it grants strictly more than the
`status: complete` this list already refuses — and the enumeration was never
updated. The loader enforces four; the ADR described three.

The larger omission: every constraint in that list bounds an overlay's
STANDING (may it call itself complete, may it relabel a package as stdlib) and
none of them bounds its INFLUENCE on a verdict. A row is not a detection-only
grant. A classified call is what `examined` MEANS, so a row also decides
whether a must_not_exist claim over a DIFFERENT boundary may be confirmed, and
a row carrying the wrong boundary yields an examined call producing no chain
for the boundary claimed. Recorded with the three-way measurement and its
control:

    no overlay                          inconclusive  rc 2
    requests.post declared net_send     violated      rc 1   <- control
    requests.post declared fs_read      CONFIRMED     rc 0

Stated as a disclosure, not an argument for refusing rows: refusing them would
close the case §55 exists for, and it is not a regression — before INV-buzab,
row PRESENCE alone permitted the whole module on weaker evidence. The residual
is that a verdict records nothing about which catalogue it trusted; that is
INV-zosun, and the indicated first move there is disclosure, not restriction.

Edited in place per docs/adr/README.md; no supersession, Status unchanged.

